Then apply these fixes:

## Fix 1: Trap dispatch — distinguish synchronous exceptions from interrupts

In rv1126b_irq_dispatch.c, the current code strips bit 31 and treats cause==11 as external interrupt. But RISC-V M-mode ecall has mcause=11 with bit31=0 (synchronous exception), not an interrupt. NuttX uses ecall for context switching.

Change `riscv_dispatch_irq()` to:
```c
#define MCAUSE_INTERRUPT  (1ul << 31)

void *riscv_dispatch_irq(uintptr_t mcause, uintreg_t *regs)
{
  if (mcause & MCAUSE_INTERRUPT)
    {
      /* Interrupt */
      uintptr_t cause = mcause & ~MCAUSE_INTERRUPT;
      if (cause == IRQ_M_TIMER)
        {
          up_timer_int();
        }
      else if (cause == IRQ_M_EXT)
        {
          int irq;
          rv1126b_ipic_soi();
          while ((irq = rv1126b_get_active_irq()) >= 0)
            {
              regs = riscv_doirq(irq, regs);
            }
          rv1126b_ipic_eoi();
        }
      else
        {
          /* Unexpected interrupt, marker and continue */
          riscv_lowputc('?');
        }
    }
  else
    {
      /* Synchronous exception — pass to NuttX common handler */
      riscv_exception((int)mcause, regs, NULL);
    }
  return regs;
}
```

## Fix 2: Timer ISR — call nxsched_process_timer()

In rv1126b_timerisr.c, add `#include <nuttx/sched.h>` if needed, then in `up_timer_int()` add `nxsched_process_timer()` after advancing mtimecmp:

```c
void up_timer_int(void)
{
  uint64_t mtime;
  uint64_t mtimecmp;

  clear_csr(mie, MIP_MTIP);

  /* Read current mtime and advance compare from it (not from old mtimecmp) */
  mtime = (uint64_t)getreg32(CTIMER_MTIMEH) << 32;
  mtime |= getreg32(CTIMER_MTIME);
  mtimecmp = mtime + g_mtimecmp_step;

  putreg32((uint32_t)(mtimecmp & 0xffffffff), CTIMER_MTIMECMP);
  putreg32((uint32_t)(mtimecmp >> 32), CTIMER_MTIMECMPH);

  set_csr(mie, MIP_MTIP);

  /* Process the OS tick */
  nxsched_process_timer();
}
```

Also add a one-time marker: static int g_tick_count = 0; if (g_tick_count < 3) { riscv_lowputc('!'); g_tick_count++; }

## Fix 3: GPLL lock bit — check correct register/bit

In rv1126b_clockconfig.c, the SDK confirms GPLL lock is at GPLL_CON[2] bit 10 (TOPCRU + 0x28 bit 10), NOT GPLL_CON[0] bit 31.

Change:
```c
#define GPLL_CON0_ADDR        (TOPCRU_BASE + 0x0020)
#define GPLL_CON0_LOCK_SHIFT  31
#define GPLL_CON0_LOCK_MASK   (1 << GPLL_CON0_LOCK_SHIFT)
```
To:
```c
/* SDK-verified: GPLL lock status is at GPLL_CON[2] bit 10 */
#define GPLL_LOCK_ADDR        (TOPCRU_BASE + 0x0028)
#define GPLL_LOCK_SHIFT       10
#define GPLL_LOCK_MASK        (1 << GPLL_LOCK_SHIFT)
```

And update `rv1126b_wait_pll_lock()` and `rv1126b_ensure_gpll_locked()` to use the new names.

## Fix 4: Post-G diagnostic markers

Add markers in these functions (just riscv_lowputc calls):

In rv1126b_timerisr.c `up_timer_initialize()`: print 'T' at entry, 't' at end.
In rv1126b_serial.c `riscv_serialinit()`: print 'S' at entry, 's' after /dev/console, '0' after /dev/ttyS0.
In rv1126b_allocateheap.c `riscv_addregion()`: print 'H'.

